# Reports

The reports look back at your data over a period of time. Each report answers
a different question, from "how was my glucose in the last two weeks?" to "what
happens after I change my infusion site?".

<Video id="overview" />

## The reports page

To open the reports, select **Reports** in the app navigation. The reports page
shows a summary of the last 14 days at the top:

- **Time in range**: a bar that shows how much of your time was in each glucose
  range. The consensus target is at least 70% of time in range, 70-180 mg/dL. If
  you set a personal target range, the caption under the bar also shows your
  time in that range.
- **Average glucose**, **eHbA1c**, **Coefficient of variation** and **Time below
  range** for the same period.
- **Typical day**: your glucose over 24 hours, with all the days of the period
  laid on top of each other. Select **Full report** to open the
  [Glucose Profile (AGP)](/docs/reports/agp).

Under the summary, the reports are in four groups: **The Big Picture**,
**Patterns & Trends**, **Lifestyle Impact** and **Treatment Insights**. Select a
report to open it. A report marked **Coming soon** is not available yet.

The app shows glucose in mg/dL by default. If you chose mmol/L in your
settings, the reports show mmol/L, and so do the targets and thresholds.

## Set the date range

Most reports show a period that you choose. Each report opens on its own
default period. For most reports this is the last 14 days.

1. At the top of the report, select the date range (for example, **Last 14
   days**). On a small screen, select **Filters**.
2. Under **Quick Selection**, select a period: **Today**, **3 Days**, **7
   Days**, **14 Days**, **30 Days** or **90 Days**.
3. To choose other dates, select the first and the last day in the **Custom
   Date Range** calendar.
4. Select **Apply Filters**. The report loads again for the new period.

The period you choose stays in place when you open another report. To go back
to the default period of the report, open the date range and select **Reset**.

Some reports have their own period controls, so they do not show the date
range: [Comparison](/docs/reports/comparison),
[Year Overview](/docs/reports/year-overview),
[Day in Review](/docs/reports/day-in-review),
[Estimated HbA1c](/docs/reports/ehba1c) and
[Data Quality](/docs/reports/data-quality).

## Print a report

Select **Print** at the top of a report, or press Ctrl+P (Cmd+P on a Mac). The
app loads the complete report before it prints. The printed page shows the
report name and the period.

## Which report to use

### Glucose

- **[Executive Summary](/docs/reports/executive-summary)**: how did I do in this
  period? All the key numbers on one page.
- **[Glucose Profile (AGP)](/docs/reports/agp)**: what does a typical day look
  like?
- **[Glucose Distribution](/docs/reports/glucose-distribution)**: how much time
  do I spend in each glucose range, and at which hours?
- **[Hourly Patterns](/docs/reports/hourly-stats)**: which hours of the day go
  well, and which are harder?
- **[Readings](/docs/reports/readings)**: what happened on each day, reading by
  reading?
- **[Day in Review](/docs/reports/day-in-review)**: what happened on one day,
  and what did my loop do?
- **[Week to Week](/docs/reports/week-to-week)**: how does each day of the week
  compare?
- **[Comparison](/docs/reports/comparison)**: what changed between two periods?
- **[Year Overview](/docs/reports/year-overview)**: how did my whole year go?
- **[Estimated HbA1c](/docs/reports/ehba1c)**: what would a lab HbA1c test
  read, and how has it changed?

### Insulin and loop

- **[Treatment Log](/docs/reports/treatments)**: every dose, carb entry, note
  and device event, in a list you can search and edit.
- **[Insulin Delivery](/docs/reports/insulin-delivery)**: how much insulin do I
  use, and how is it split between basal and bolus?
- **[Basal Rate Analysis](/docs/reports/basal-analysis)**: how does my basal
  rate change through the day?
- **[Insulin Dosing Profile](/docs/reports/idp)**: my insulin and my glucose on
  one page, for a clinic visit.
- **[Site Change Impact](/docs/reports/site-change-impact)**: what does my
  glucose do around an infusion site change?
- **[Loop Analysis](/docs/reports/loop-analysis)**: what does my loop change,
  when, and what occurs after it?

### Devices and data

- **[Data Quality](/docs/reports/data-quality)**: which readings may come from
  the sensor and not from my glucose?
- **[Battery](/docs/reports/battery)**: how long do the batteries of my devices
  last?

### Activity and sleep

- **[Step Count](/docs/reports/steps)**: how active am I, and what does my
  glucose do at the same time?
- **[Heart Rate](/docs/reports/heart-rate)**: what does my heart rate do
  through the day?
- **[Sleep & Overnight](/docs/reports/sleep)**: how do I sleep, and what does my
  glucose do overnight?

## Good to know

- **Only the reports you can read.** On a public share link, or as a member
  with limited access, you see only the reports for the data you are allowed
  to read. The Insulin Dosing Profile is not available on a public share link.
- **The last 24 hours only.** If the person who shared the data limited you to
  the last 24 hours, a notice at the top says so. The reports then show no
  data older than 24 hours, whatever period you choose.
- **Reliability badge.** Some reports show a badge such as "Based on 9 days of
  data (14 recommended)". The badge shows when the period has too few days
  with readings. For a period of 14 days or more, the report needs readings on
  at least 14 days. For a shorter period, it needs readings on every day. Treat
  the figures with care until you have more data.

<Callout type="warning" title="Reports are not medical advice">

  The reports show what happened in your data. They do not tell you why, or
  what to change. Meals, activity, sleep, illness and sensor problems can all
  change the figures. Discuss any pattern with your diabetes care team before
  you change your treatment.

</Callout>
